schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Source system
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A living knowledge environment (wiki, repo, research database, forum,
    digital garden) — not a single document or a static link — that has a
    steward, receives ongoing additions, and offers a return path to credit
    and trace material back to its origin.
  source_traditions:
    - digital-garden practice (Maggie Appleton)
    - commons governance (Elinor Ostrom's design principles)
    - open-source repo/maintainer conventions
  toolkit_usage: >-
    The Regen Knowledge Commons Toolkit's Resource Graph layer is explicitly
    framed as "not a link list" but the place the rest of the toolkit stays
    grounded in reality; the three-question test (Who tends this? Is new
    material still added? Can I trace and credit it?) determines whether a
    dependency is a source system or a rotting link.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - "link list / resources page (a flat, unowned, unmaintained set of URLs — explicitly NOT a source system)"
    - "Resource Graph vs. Ecosystem Atlas (storage-with-lineage vs. the interpretive lens layered on top of it)"
  important_distinctions:
    - "a source system has three required properties: steward, ongoing additions, return path to credit — a source lacking any one of these is a link, not a system"
    - "inclusion does not mean endorsement — adding a source system as a reference is not the project vouching for its content"
  tensions:
    - "openness to add broadly vs. the discipline of marking maturity honestly so raw inclusions don't silently become 'true'"
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating any URL you cite as a "source system" without a steward or
    return path — this collapses the distinction the whole concept exists to
    preserve, and lets link rot back in under a new name.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/source-systems.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
